// same reason: a routing policy is AUTHORED, then made live, then superseded.
//
//   DRAFT     editable, never resolved at runtime. This is what the dry-run
//             (`/simulate`) endpoint replays recorded traffic against.
//   ACTIVE    the row the runtime resolver serves for its (tenant, taskKey).
//   ARCHIVED  a superseded revision, kept addressable for one-click rollback
//             and for the HIPAA §164.312(b) before/after audit trail (§3A.8).
//
// Supersede-only: an ACTIVE row is not edited in place, it is replaced by a
// higher `policyVersion` and demoted to ARCHIVED. That is why `policyVersion`
// is a column of its own and is NOT the `_version` OCC counter.
enum AiRoutingPolicyStatus {
  DRAFT
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED

  @@schema("core")
}

// TASK-818 §3A.3 — how the router picks among the candidates of one rank tier.
// The vocabulary is the common denominator of the gateways surveyed in §3A.2
// (LiteLLM Router, Portkey, Envoy AI Gateway, Kong AI Proxy Advanced,
// OpenRouter, Cloudflare AI Gateway).
//
//   PRIORITY        strict candidate order; `rank` decides, weights ignored.
//   WEIGHTED        weighted split WITHIN a rank tier.
//   LEAST_BUSY      fewest in-flight requests.
//   LOWEST_LATENCY  best observed TTFT.
//   LOWEST_COST     cheapest per the `AiPriceBook` row effective now.
//
// Deliberately NOT a member: any learned / prompt-difficulty strategy. §3A.6(4)
// rules quality-aware routing out for day one — the space has no reproducible
// evaluation yet, so a member here would be a promise the resolver cannot keep.
enum AiRoutingStrategy {
  PRIORITY
  WEIGHTED
  LEAST_BUSY
  LOWEST_LATENCY
  LOWEST_COST

  @@schema("core")
}

// TASK-818 §3A.4 — what happens when a request NAMES a provider and that
// provider is down. The platform default is STRICT, which deliberately inverts
// the industry default (OpenRouter and Cloudflare default `allow_fallbacks` to
// true; LiteLLM fires configured fallbacks by default).
//
//   STRICT                  503 `provider_unavailable`. No silent substitution.
//   STRICT_UNLESS_OPTED_IN  hard-fail unless the request itself carried
//                           `allow_fallbacks: true`.
//   POLICY_MAY_OVERRIDE     the policy chain may re-route without the caller
//                           asking.
//
// The justification is clinical, not stylistic: BAA coverage is per-vendor AND
// per-model, so one fallback hop can move PHI outside BAA coverage; RFC 9111
// obsoleted the `Warning` header, so there is no standard way to tell a caller
// a substitution happened; and under BYOK a hop onto a SYSTEM credential
// silently flips the metering class. Whatever this mode says, the three hard
// gates (same residency class, BAA-covered target, same funding tier) are
// enforced in CODE, not in policy text — a hop crossing any of them is a
// rejection, not a fallback.
enum AiExplicitProviderMode {
  STRICT
  STRICT_UNLESS_OPTED_IN
  POLICY_MAY_OVERRIDE

  @@schema("core")
}
